CHIMERA

Reassemble, test, adapt, preserve

The important part is the coupling: memory affects planning, criticism improves execution, and feedback changes the next attempt. That makes the combination potentially more useful than the isolated parts. Whether it actually is more useful must be measured.

Built and tested: Chimera v0.1

I implemented a single-file Python prototype and ran it successfully, including a test where the critic rejects an initial plan and forces the system to replan.

Chimera v0.1 — Python engine

16 KB · Standard library only · Local execution

Example mission output

Execution trace and operator records

I gave it a practical mission: develop an original fantasy creature illustration with expressive brushwork and dramatic lighting.

It produced Thornback, a six-legged, moss-antlered creature crossing a massive tree-root bridge above a fog-filled landscape, including composition, anatomy, color direction, and a reusable illustration prompt.

To connect it to a local language model:

ollama pull llama3.2
python chimera_v01.py --backend ollama --model llama3.2 --mission "Develop an original fantasy creature"

The tested offline mode uses deterministic mock responses; the optional local-model mode allows the components to generate new content. I haven’t tested that local-model integration in this environment.

Suggested GitHub path: PANTHEON/MODULES/chimera_v01.py. It hasn’t been committed to your repository.

This is the first executable component, not a finished autonomous intelligence. But it establishes the mechanism we wanted:

Clip capabilities. Compose them. Execute a task. Challenge the outcome. Revise. Preserve what works.

Now Chimera has a working foundation we can actually develop against.